Operator Overloading via Traits — Built-in Infix Operators
===========================================================

Per reference §"Operator Overloading via Traits":

| Operator | Trait | Method | Signature |
|---|---|---|---|
| `<` `<=` `>` `>=` | `Ord` | `lt` `le` `gt` `ge` | `(T, T) -> bool` |
| `==` `!=` | `Eq` | `eq` `ne` | `(T, T) -> bool` |
| `+` | `Add` | `add` | `(T, T) -> T` |
| `-` | `Sub` | `sub` | `(T, T) -> T` |
| `*` | `Mul` | `mul` | `(T, T) -> T` |
| `/` | `Div` | `div` | `(T, T) -> T` |

- Operators on user-defined struct/enum types desugar to trait
  method calls.
- The compiler maps each operator to a fixed `(trait, method)` pair.
- Built-in numeric operators on `int`, `f64`, etc. are unaffected.
- Dispatch through a trait applies whenever **at least one** operand
  is a user-defined named type and a matching `impl` exists.
- Mixed-operand impls (e.g. `Mul[int, Vec3, Vec3]`) let primitives
  appear on either side.

This file pins:

- Trait `Add` on user struct → `+` works
- Sub, Mul, Div on user struct
- Mixed-operand `Mul[int, Vec3, Vec3]` — primitive on the LHS
- Ord on user struct → `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`
- Eq on user struct → `==`, `!=`
- Composition: `a + b - c` desugars correctly

File-local prefix `Ooi` (Operator-Overload-Infix).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


A 2D vector struct
-------------------

    struct OoiVec2
        x: int
        y: int


### `+` via `Add` trait

    trait OoiAdd[T]
        ooi_add(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl OoiAdd[OoiVec2]
        ooi_add(a: OoiVec2, b: OoiVec2) -> OoiVec2 = OoiVec2(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y)

    #test
    test_ooi_add_via_trait() -> unit
        val a = OoiVec2(1, 2)
        val b = OoiVec2(10, 20)
        val c = OoiAdd.ooi_add(a, b)
        assert(c.x == 11 && c.y == 22, s"got (${c.x}, ${c.y})")


### `+` desugaring on user struct via the canonical `Add[T]` trait

The reference's headline example: `c = a + b` desugars to
`Add.add(a, b) → Add_add_Vec2(a, b)`.

    trait Add[T]
        add(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl Add[OoiVec2]
        add(a: OoiVec2, b: OoiVec2) -> OoiVec2 = OoiVec2(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y)

    #test
    test_ooi_plus_desugar() -> unit
        val a = OoiVec2(1, 2)
        val b = OoiVec2(10, 20)
        val c = a + b
        assert(c.x == 11 && c.y == 22, s"got (${c.x}, ${c.y})")


### `-` via `Sub` trait

    trait Sub[T]
        sub(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl Sub[OoiVec2]
        sub(a: OoiVec2, b: OoiVec2) -> OoiVec2 = OoiVec2(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y)

    #test
    test_ooi_minus_desugar() -> unit
        val a = OoiVec2(10, 20)
        val b = OoiVec2(3, 4)
        val c = a - b
        assert(c.x == 7 && c.y == 16, s"got (${c.x}, ${c.y})")


### `*` via `Mul` trait on a user struct (component-wise)

    trait Mul[T]
        mul(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl Mul[OoiVec2]
        mul(a: OoiVec2, b: OoiVec2) -> OoiVec2 = OoiVec2(a.x * b.x, a.y * b.y)

    #test
    test_ooi_times_desugar() -> unit
        val a = OoiVec2(2, 3)
        val b = OoiVec2(5, 7)
        val c = a * b
        assert(c.x == 10 && c.y == 21, s"got (${c.x}, ${c.y})")


### `/` via `Div` trait on a user struct (component-wise)

    trait Div[T]
        div(a: T, b: T) -> T

    impl Div[OoiVec2]
        div(a: OoiVec2, b: OoiVec2) -> OoiVec2 = OoiVec2(a.x / b.x, a.y / b.y)

    #test
    test_ooi_div_desugar() -> unit
        val a = OoiVec2(20, 30)
        val b = OoiVec2(4, 5)
        val c = a / b
        assert(c.x == 5 && c.y == 6, s"got (${c.x}, ${c.y})")


Composition: `a + b - c` chains desugared operators
----------------------------------------------------

### Add and Sub combine cleanly

    #test
    test_ooi_chain() -> unit
        val a = OoiVec2(10, 20)
        val b = OoiVec2(3, 4)
        val c = OoiVec2(2, 5)
        val r = a + b - c
        assert(r.x == 11 && r.y == 19, s"got (${r.x}, ${r.y})")


Mixed-operand `Mul` — primitive on the LHS
-------------------------------------------

Per the reference, `3 * Vec3(1,2,4)` dispatches via
`Mul[int, Vec3, Vec3]`. The reverse needs its own impl — there is
no implicit symmetry.

    struct OoiVec3
        x: int
        y: int
        z: int

    trait OoiScale[A, B, R]
        ooi_smul(a: A, b: B) -> R

    impl OoiScale[int, OoiVec3, OoiVec3]
        ooi_smul(s: int, v: OoiVec3) -> OoiVec3 = OoiVec3(s * v.x, s * v.y, s * v.z)

    #test
    test_ooi_mixed_operand() -> unit
        val v = OoiVec3(1, 2, 4)
        val r = OoiScale.ooi_smul(3, v)
        assert(r.x == 3 && r.y == 6 && r.z == 12, s"got (${r.x}, ${r.y}, ${r.z})")


Ord on a user struct via `<` `<=` `>` `>=`
-------------------------------------------

The reference says Ord maps to the four order operators. We compare
two `OoiBox` values by their `.v` field.

    struct OoiBox
        v: int

    trait Ord[T]
        lt(a: T, b: T) -> bool
        le(a: T, b: T) -> bool
        gt(a: T, b: T) -> bool
        ge(a: T, b: T) -> bool

    impl Ord[OoiBox]
        lt(a: OoiBox, b: OoiBox) -> bool = a.v < b.v
        le(a: OoiBox, b: OoiBox) -> bool = a.v <= b.v
        gt(a: OoiBox, b: OoiBox) -> bool = a.v > b.v
        ge(a: OoiBox, b: OoiBox) -> bool = a.v >= b.v

    #test
    test_ooi_ord_operators() -> unit
        val a = OoiBox(3)
        val b = OoiBox(5)
        val c = OoiBox(3)
        assert(a < b, "3 < 5")
        assert(!(b < a), "5 not < 3")
        assert(a <= c, "3 <= 3")
        assert(b > a, "5 > 3")
        assert(c >= a, "3 >= 3")


Eq on a user struct via `==` `!=`
----------------------------------

    trait Eq[T]
        eq(a: T, b: T) -> bool
        ne(a: T, b: T) -> bool

    impl Eq[OoiBox]
        eq(a: OoiBox, b: OoiBox) -> bool = a.v == b.v
        ne(a: OoiBox, b: OoiBox) -> bool = a.v != b.v

    #test
    test_ooi_eq_operators() -> unit
        val a = OoiBox(7)
        val b = OoiBox(7)
        val c = OoiBox(9)
        assert(a == b, "7 == 7")
        assert(!(a == c), "7 not == 9")
        assert(a != c, "7 != 9")
        assert(!(a != b), "7 not != 7")
